## Limiting Access to cloud-init

The cloud-init server gives each node its configuration, including the munge
key, and its admin API changes that configuration for every node. As installed,
any user who can reach the head node can read it. The steps below let only root
read `/cloud-init`, on a compute node or the head node, and only the head node
use `/cloud-init/admin`. This assumes that ports below 1024 stay root-only on
the head and compute nodes, and that root on any other host on the provisioning
network is trusted.

First keep ports below 1024 root-only on the head node, and collect the head
node's addresses for the rules below. `ochami` connects to
`https://${sms_name}:8443`, so the rules cover every IPv4 address the name
resolves to as well as `${sms_ip}`; they don't cover IPv6, so the step stops if
the name resolves to an IPv6 address.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Keep ports below 1024 root-only on the head node -->
```bash
# Collect the head node's IPv4 addresses
if getent ahosts "${sms_name}" | awk '{print $1}' | grep -q ':'; then
	echo "ERROR: ${sms_name} resolves to IPv6; use IPv4 only for it" >&2
	exit 1
fi
sms_head_addrs=$( (echo "${sms_ip}"; getent ahostsv4 "${sms_name}" \
	| awk '{print $1}') | sort -u | paste -sd, -)
# Keep ports below 1024 root-only
echo "net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start = 1024" \
	> /etc/sysctl.d/90-ohpc-privports.conf
sysctl -q -p /etc/sysctl.d/90-ohpc-privports.conf
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Then add the access rules to haproxy, which publishes cloud-init on ports 8081
and 8443 of the head node. podman's published ports keep the client's address
and source port, so haproxy sees them as the client sent them. The rules go in
the `openchami` frontend, next to its `/cloud-init` path rule.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Limit cloud-init to privileged source ports and its admin API to the head -->
```bash
# Limit cloud-init to privileged source ports and its admin API to the head
haproxy_cfg=/etc/openchami/configs/haproxy.cfg
acl_file=/opt/ohpc/admin/haproxy/cloud-init-access.cfg
mkdir -p /opt/ohpc/admin/haproxy
cat > "${acl_file}" <<EOF
  # OpenHPC: /cloud-init only from privileged source ports, and
  # /cloud-init/admin only from the head node
  acl SRC_privport src_port 1:1023
  acl SRC_head src ${sms_ip}
  acl PATH_cloud-init-admin path_beg -i /cloud-init/admin
  http-request deny deny_status 403 if PATH_cloud-init !SRC_privport
  http-request deny deny_status 403 if PATH_cloud-init-admin !SRC_head
EOF
if ! grep -q 'acl SRC_privport' "${haproxy_cfg}"; then
	sed -i "/^ *acl PATH_cloud-init path_beg/r ${acl_file}" "${haproxy_cfg}"
fi
grep -q 'acl SRC_privport' "${haproxy_cfg}" \
	|| { echo "ERROR: cloud-init access rules not in ${haproxy_cfg}" >&2; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Check the configuration and reload haproxy in place with `SIGUSR2`; restarting
`haproxy.service` would restart every service in `openchami.target`. Those
services can still be restarting right after they start, so continue once the
check and the reload succeed:

```bash
# Check the configuration, then reload haproxy
podman exec haproxy haproxy -c -q -f /usr/local/etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg \
	&& podman kill --signal USR2 haproxy
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Check and reload haproxy once its container stays up -->
<!-- ohpc_command for ((t=0; ; t++)); do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     podman exec haproxy haproxy -c -q -f /usr/local/etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command         && podman kill --signal USR2 haproxy >/dev/null && break -->
<!-- ohpc_command     (( t < 12 )) || { echo "haproxy: reload FAILED"; exit 1; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "Waiting for haproxy..." -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 5 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_command echo "haproxy: reloaded" -->
